description:
## Background

Rheo has a reference-based integration test suite in `crates/tests/` that snapshot-compares HTML/PDF/EPUB outputs. There is currently no mechanism to verify that real-world Rheo projects continue to compile as the codebase evolves. This issue adds the plumbing for a new 'compatibility test' layer.

## Goal

Create infrastructure that can clone a public GitHub repo, patch its rheo.toml version field, run `rheo compile` against it, and assert the exit code is 0. All compat tests must be gated behind a `RUN_COMPAT_TESTS=1` environment variable (consistent with the existing `RUN_HTML_TESTS=1`, `RUN_PDF_TESTS=1`, `RUN_EPUB_TESTS=1` gates).

## Files to create/modify

### 1. `crates/tests/src/helpers/remote.rs` (NEW)

Implement three public functions:

```rust
/// Clone a public GitHub repo using `git clone --depth 1`.
/// Destination: `crates/tests/store/compat/<name>/`.
/// If the destination already exists, skip cloning (fast local re-runs).
/// Returns the path to the cloned directory.
pub fn clone_repo(url: &str, name: &str) -> PathBuf

/// Patch the `version` field in `<project>/rheo.toml` to match
/// env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"). Overrides whatever version the external
/// project declares, so version-mismatch errors don't mask real failures.
/// Does nothing if no rheo.toml is present.
pub fn patch_rheo_version(project_path: &Path)

/// Clone the repo, patch its version, run `rheo compile <project_path>`,
/// and panic with full stdout+stderr if exit code is non-zero.
pub fn run_compat(url: &str, name: &str)
```

Implementation notes:
- `clone_repo`: use `std::process::Command` to run `git clone --depth 1 <url> <dest>`. Compute dest as `PathBuf::from(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR")).join("store/compat").join(name)`. If dest already exists, return it immediately.
- `patch_rheo_version`: read rheo.toml with `std::fs::read_to_string`, replace the `version = "..."` line using a regex or simple string replacement, write back with `std::fs::write`. Reference: the version-injection logic in `crates/tests/src/helpers/test_store.rs` — but this must *override* an existing value, not just inject a missing one.
- `run_compat`: calls clone_repo, then patch_rheo_version, then builds the rheo binary path using `env!("CARGO_BIN_EXE_rheo")` (same mechanism as `crates/tests/tests/harness.rs`). Runs `rheo compile <cloned_path>`. Sets `TYPST_IGNORE_SYSTEM_FONTS=1` on the command. On non-zero exit, panics with a message containing full stdout and stderr.

### 2. `crates/tests/src/helpers/mod.rs` (MODIFY)

Add `pub mod remote;` alongside existing module declarations.

### 3. `crates/tests/tests/compat.rs` (NEW)

Create the test binary skeleton including the `smoke_tests!` macro definition, ready for repo entries to be added (by rheo-3cr):

```rust
use rheo_tests::helpers::remote::run_compat;

fn compat_enabled() -> bool {
    std::env::var("RUN_COMPAT_TESTS").as_deref() == Ok("1")
}

macro_rules! smoke_tests {
    ( $( ($name:ident, $url:expr) ),* $(,)? ) => {
        $(
            ::paste::paste! {
                #[test]
                fn [<smoke_ $name>]() {
                    if !compat_enabled() { return; }
                    run_compat($url, stringify!($name));
                }
            }
        )*
    };
}

// Repos are registered in rheo-3cr
smoke_tests! {}
```

The macro takes `(name, url)` entries — two fields only. The function name `smoke_<name>` is generated automatically. The `name` identifier is the repo slug (last URL path segment with `.` replaced by `_`), which is trivially derivable from the URL without a separate choice.

### 4. `crates/tests/Cargo.toml` (MODIFY)

Add the new test binary entry and the `paste` dev-dependency (needed for identifier concatenation in the macro):

```toml
[[test]]
name = "compat"
path = "tests/compat.rs"
```

```toml
[dev-dependencies]
paste = "1"
```

## Reference files

- `crates/tests/src/helpers/test_store.rs` — version injection pattern
- `crates/tests/tests/harness.rs` — RUN_* env var gating, TYPST_IGNORE_SYSTEM_FONTS, rheo binary invocation

## Acceptance criteria

- `cargo build --test compat` passes with no warnings
- `cargo test --test compat` (without RUN_COMPAT_TESTS=1) completes immediately with 0 tests run
- `run_compat` is callable from test code

## Background

This is part of a feature to auto-detect Rheo packages from Typst import statements. After resolving a package import to a local directory (see companion issue for find_local_package_dir), this issue reads the package's typst.toml manifest and converts any [tool.rheo.{format}] section into a PackageAssets value that flows into the existing resolve_assets() pipeline.

The [tool.rheo.html] section in a package's typst.toml looks like:
```toml
[tool.rheo.html]
js_scripts = "dist/lib.js"
css_stylesheet = "index.css"
```

Field names must exactly match the asset config keys the plugin expects (css_stylesheet singular, js_scripts plural — see HTML plugin constants at crates/html/src/lib.rs). Paths are relative to the package's source_root and will be resolved against it by the existing resolve_assets() machinery in crates/cli/src/lib.rs.

dest is set to "{namespace}/{name}" (e.g. "rheo/slides"). No wildcard copy glob is added.

## Relevant existing code

- `crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs:259-264` — `PackageAssets { assets: PluginAssets, source_root: PathBuf }`
- `crates/core/src/config.rs:36-56` — `PluginAssets { copy: Vec<String>, dest: Option<String>, extra: toml::Table }`
- `crates/core/src/plugins/manifest.rs` — `ImportedPackage` defined in companion issues
- `crates/cli/src/lib.rs:459-621` — `resolve_assets()` reads `package.assets.extra` values and resolves them relative to `package.source_root`
- `crates/html/src/lib.rs:88-101` — HTML plugin declares asset config names: `"css_stylesheet"` and `"js_scripts"`

## Steps to implement

1. In `crates/core/src/plugins/manifest.rs`, add:

```rust
use crate::config::PluginAssets;
use crate::plugins::PackageAssets;

/// Reads {source_root}/typst.toml and returns a PackageAssets for the given format_name
/// if [tool.rheo.{format_name}] exists and is non-empty. Returns None otherwise.
pub fn manifest_package_assets(pkg: &ImportedPackage, format_name: &str) -> Option<PackageAssets> {
    let manifest_path = pkg.source_root.join("typst.toml");
    let content = std::fs::read_to_string(&manifest_path).ok()?;
    let toml: toml::Value = toml::from_str(&content).ok()?;
    let section = toml
        .get("tool")?
        .get("rheo")?
        .get(format_name)?
        .as_table()?;
    if section.is_empty() {
        return None;
    }
    let extra: toml::map::Map<String, toml::Value> = section.clone().into_iter().collect();
    Some(PackageAssets {
        assets: PluginAssets {
            copy: vec![],
            dest: Some(format!("{}/{}", pkg.namespace, pkg.name)),
            extra,
        },
        source_root: pkg.source_root.clone(),
    })
}

/// Scans import_paths, locates each package locally, reads its manifest,
/// and returns PackageAssets for format_name. Silently skips packages that
/// are not found locally or have no [tool.rheo.{format_name}] section.
/// already_declared are raw import path strings from plugin_section.packages()
/// that should not be auto-detected (to prevent duplicates).
pub fn detect_manifest_package_assets(
    import_paths: &[String],
    format_name: &str,
    already_declared: &[String],
) -> Vec<PackageAssets> {
    import_paths
        .iter()
        .filter(|p| !already_declared.contains(p))
        .filter_map(|p| find_local_package_dir(p))
        .filter_map(|pkg| manifest_package_assets(&pkg, format_name))
        .collect()
}
```

2. Export `manifest_package_assets` and `detect_manifest_package_assets` from `crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs`.

3. Add unit tests using a temp dir:
   - Test that a typst.toml with [tool.rheo.html] produces PackageAssets with correct dest, extra, and empty copy
   - Test that a typst.toml with no [tool.rheo] section returns None
   - Test that a missing typst.toml returns None
   - Test that an empty [tool.rheo.html] section returns None
   - Test detect_manifest_package_assets skips packages in already_declared list

## Expected outcome

Given a package at /tmp/testpkg/ with typst.toml containing [tool.rheo.html] { css_stylesheet = "style.css" }, manifest_package_assets returns PackageAssets with dest="testns/testpkg", extra={"css_stylesheet": "style.css"}, copy=[], source_root=/tmp/testpkg/.

## Background

In `BuiltSpine::build()` (`crates/core/src/reticulate/spine.rs:34-92`), each spine file is processed by a private `transform_source()` helper (lines 96-116). That helper constructs a fresh `LinkTransformer` on every call (lines 107-113):

```rust
let transformer = if ext_name == "pdf" && spine_files.len() > 1 {
    LinkTransformer::new(ext_name).with_spine(spine_files.to_vec())
} else {
    LinkTransformer::new(ext_name)
};
```

Inside `LinkTransformer::compute_transformations()` (`crates/core/src/reticulate/transformer.rs:90-163`), `build_label_map(spine)` (line 97-100) constructs a `HashMap<String, String>` from the spine file list on every call. For a 50-file merged PDF spine, this is 50 identical HashMaps built and discarded.

Since `spine_files` and `ext_name` are constant across all iterations of the loop (lines 51-77 of spine.rs), the `LinkTransformer` (and its label map) should be created once.

## Files

- **`crates/core/src/reticulate/spine.rs`** — `BuiltSpine::build()` lines 34-92, private `transform_source()` lines 96-116
- **`crates/core/src/reticulate/transformer.rs`** — `LinkTransformer` struct and impl

## Steps

1. In `BuiltSpine::build()` (`spine.rs`), move the transformer construction to before the `for spine_file in &spine_files` loop. Create it once:
   ```rust
   let transformer = if ext_name == "pdf" && spine_files.len() > 1 {
       LinkTransformer::new(ext_name).with_spine(spine_files.to_vec())
   } else {
       LinkTransformer::new(ext_name)
   };
   ```

2. Inside the loop (where `transform_source(&source, spine_file, &spine_files, format_ext, root)?` is currently called), call the transformer directly:
   ```rust
   let transformed_source = transformer.transform_source(&source, spine_file, root)?;
   ```

3. Delete or inline the private `transform_source()` free function (lines 96-116) — it exists solely to construct the transformer and forward the call. With the transformer hoisted, it has no remaining purpose.

4. Run `cargo test` — all tests must pass.
5. Run `cargo fmt && cargo clippy -- -D warnings`.

## Expected outcome

For an N-file spine, `build_label_map` is called once instead of N times, and one `LinkTransformer` is constructed instead of N. No behaviour change — the transformer configuration is identical across all iterations.

## Background

`crates/core/src/reticulate/spine.rs` contains two nearly-identical implementations of the same glob-expansion logic:

1. **`SpineOptions::generate()`** (lines 201-232) — method on the spine config struct, expands `vertebrae` glob patterns or returns all `.typ` files
2. **`generate_spine()`** (lines 235-277) — free function, duplicates the same logic, adding only a `require_spine: bool` guard at the top

`generate_spine` was likely written before `SpineOptions::generate` was added (or vice versa), leaving two diverging sources of truth. At least ~40 lines are pure duplication.

Additionally, `SpineOptions::generate()` and `generate_spine()` both call `collect_all_typst_files` for the empty-vertebrae case, and both call `collect_one_typst_file` for the `None` case — but `generate_spine` partially re-implements the `Some(spine)` branch rather than delegating.

## Files

- **`crates/core/src/reticulate/spine.rs`** — entire file, particularly lines 197-277

## Steps

1. Refactor `generate_spine()` to delegate to `SpineOptions::generate()` instead of re-implementing:
   ```rust
   pub fn generate_spine(
       root: &Path,
       spine_config: Option<&SpineOptions>,
       require_spine: bool,
   ) -> Result<Vec<PathBuf>> {
       if require_spine && spine_config.is_none() {
           return Err(RheoError::project_config(
               "spine configuration required but not provided",
           ));
       }
       match spine_config {
           None => collect_one_typst_file(root),
           Some(spine) => spine.generate(root),
       }
   }
   ```

2. Delete the duplicated glob-expansion code from `generate_spine` (the `Some(spine) if spine.vertebrae.is_empty()` and `Some(spine)` match arms).

3. Verify that `SpineOptions::generate()` handles all cases correctly: empty vertebrae (all files), non-empty vertebrae (glob expansion), and the sorting behaviour. The existing unit tests for `generate_spine` (lines 279-437) cover these — confirm they still pass.

4. Run `cargo test` — all tests must pass.
5. Run `cargo fmt && cargo clippy -- -D warnings`.

## Expected outcome

`generate_spine` delegates to `SpineOptions::generate`, removing ~40 lines of duplicated code. Single source of truth for spine file resolution.

## Background

This is part of a feature to auto-detect Rheo packages from Typst import statements. When a .typ file contains `#import "@rheo/slides:0.1.0"`, rheo should automatically check that package's typst.toml for a [tool.rheo] section and create corresponding asset blocks — without the user needing to list anything in rheo.toml.

This issue adds the first piece: a function that scans project .typ files and extracts package import strings.

## Relevant existing code

- `crates/core/src/reticulate/parser.rs:28-30` — `extract_imports(source: &Source) -> Vec<ImportInfo>` parses Typst AST and returns all import/include paths
- `crates/core/src/reticulate/types.rs:26-36` — `ImportInfo` struct: `path: String`, `byte_range`, `is_package: bool`
- `crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs` — existing plugin utilities; add new `manifest` submodule here

## Steps to implement

1. Create `crates/core/src/plugins/manifest.rs` (new file).

2. Add the following function:

```rust
use crate::reticulate::parser::extract_imports;
use std::collections::HashSet;
use std::path::Path;
use typst::syntax::Source;

/// Scans project .typ files for package imports (those starting with '@').
/// Returns deduplicated import path strings, e.g. ["@rheo/slides:0.1.0"].
/// Files that cannot be read are silently skipped.
pub fn scan_project_package_imports(typ_files: &[impl AsRef<Path>]) -> Vec<String> {
    let mut seen = HashSet::new();
    let mut result = Vec::new();
    for file in typ_files {
        let Ok(content) = std::fs::read_to_string(file.as_ref()) else { continue };
        let source = Source::detached(content);
        for import in extract_imports(&source) {
            if import.is_package && seen.insert(import.path.clone()) {
                result.push(import.path);
            }
        }
    }
    result
}
```

3. In `crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs`, add near the top:
   - `pub mod manifest;`
   - `pub use manifest::scan_project_package_imports;`

4. Ensure `crates/core/src/lib.rs` re-exports `plugins::manifest` if needed for CLI access.

5. Add unit tests in `crates/core/src/plugins/manifest.rs`:
   - Test that a .typ file containing `#import "@preview/tablex:0.0.6": tablex` returns `["@preview/tablex:0.0.6"]`
   - Test that non-package imports (relative paths like `"./utils.typ"`) are excluded
   - Test that duplicate package imports across multiple files are deduplicated
   - Test that unreadable files are silently skipped

## Expected outcome

`scan_project_package_imports(&project.typ_files)` returns a deduplicated `Vec<String>` of `@namespace/name:version` strings for every package imported across all project .typ files.

## Background

Rheo's link transformer silently skips `#link` call sites that go through wrapper functions. In `crates/core/src/reticulate/parser.rs`, `parse_link_call()` (line 37-39) only matches function calls where the identifier text equals `"link"`. Any call to a user-defined wrapper returns `None` immediately.

Example that fails:
```typst
#let chapter-ref(path, title) = link(path, title)
#chapter-ref("./ch02.typ", [Chapter 2])
```
When compiling to HTML, `"./ch02.typ"` should become `"./ch02.html"`. Instead it passes through unchanged because `parse_link_call` never recognises `chapter-ref` as a link call.

Also fails for direct aliases:
```typst
#let mylink = link
#mylink("./ch02.typ")[text]
```

The same pre-pass and `ReplaceStringLiteralInPlace` transform introduced in this issue will be reused by the follow-on issue for let-bound URL variables.

## Relevant files

- `crates/core/src/reticulate/parser.rs` — `extract_links()`, `parse_link_call()`, `extract_first_string_arg()` — all lines 1-143
- `crates/core/src/reticulate/types.rs` — `LinkInfo`, `LinkTransform` definitions
- `crates/core/src/reticulate/serializer.rs` — `apply_transformations()` dispatch on `LinkTransform` variants
- `crates/core/src/reticulate/transformer.rs` — `transform_source()` (lines 34-51) orchestrates the pipeline

## Typst AST node kinds to use

For `#let chapter-ref(path, title) = link(path, title)`:
- Outer node: `SyntaxKind::LetBinding`
  - Child `Ident("chapter-ref")`
  - Child `Closure`:
    - Child `Params`: contains `Ident` children for each param (`"path"`, `"title"`)
    - Child body (`FuncCall` or `CodeBlock` containing `FuncCall`):
      - `Ident("link")`
      - `Args`: first positional arg is `Ident("path")`

For `#let mylink = link`:
- `SyntaxKind::LetBinding`
  - Child `Ident("mylink")`
  - Child `Ident("link")` ← direct alias (no Closure wrapping)

Verify these SyntaxKind values at runtime by printing `node.kind()` — the typst-syntax 0.14.2 docs or source are authoritative.

## Steps

### Step 1 — Add `WrapperInfo` and `collect_link_wrappers()` to `parser.rs`

```rust
/// Maps a wrapper function name → the index of its parameter that is passed as the URL to link().
pub type WrapperMap = HashMap<String, usize>;  // fn_name → url_param_index

/// First-pass scan: collect all same-file function definitions that wrap link().
pub fn collect_link_wrappers(root: &SyntaxNode) -> WrapperMap {
    let mut map = HashMap::new();
    collect_wrappers_from_node(root, &mut map);
    map
}
```

`collect_wrappers_from_node` traverses the AST recursively. For each `LetBinding` node:

**Direct alias** — when the LetBinding's value child is an `Ident("link")`:
```
map.insert(binding_ident_name, 0)
```

**Closure wrapper** — when the LetBinding's value child is a `Closure`:
1. Collect the param names from the `Params` node (in order): `["path", "title"]`
2. Recursively search the closure body for a `FuncCall` where the ident is `"link"` AND the first positional arg is an `Ident` whose name appears in the param list
3. If found, record `map.insert(fn_name, param_index)` where `param_index` is the position of that param in the param list (0-based)
4. If the `link()` call's first arg is a `SyntaxKind::Str` (URL is hardcoded in the wrapper), skip — there's nothing to rewrite at the call site

Only simple patterns are supported. Skip if: the URL param is used in a conditional, the closure has a `..rest` param pack before the URL param, or the body is too complex to determine which param feeds the URL.

### Step 2 — Add `ReplaceStringLiteralInPlace` to `types.rs`

```rust
pub enum LinkTransform {
    // ... existing variants ...
    /// Replace only the quoted string literal at `byte_range` with `new_value` (re-quoted).
    /// Used when the link call cannot be fully reconstructed (e.g. wrapper functions).
    ReplaceStringLiteralInPlace { new_value: String },
}
```

### Step 3 — Handle `ReplaceStringLiteralInPlace` in `serializer.rs`

In `apply_transformations()`, add a match arm for `ReplaceStringLiteralInPlace { new_value }`:
```rust
LinkTransform::ReplaceStringLiteralInPlace { new_value } => {
    // byte_range covers the Str node including its surrounding quotes
    result.replace_range(range, &format!("\"{}\"", new_value));
}
```

### Step 4 — Extend `extract_links()` to use the wrapper map

Change the signature to:
```rust
pub fn extract_links(source: &Source) -> Vec<LinkInfo>
```
remains public and unchanged (callers unaffected). Internally it now does:
```rust
pub fn extract_links(source: &Source) -> Vec<LinkInfo> {
    let root = typst::syntax::parse(source.text());
    let wrappers = collect_link_wrappers(&root);
    let mut links = Vec::new();
    extract_links_from_node(&root, &root, &mut links, &wrappers);
    links
}
```

Update `extract_links_from_node` to accept `&WrapperMap` and pass it down.

### Step 5 — Extend `parse_link_call` to match wrapper calls

After the existing `if ident.text() != LINK_IDENT_ID` check, add a fallback:
```rust
let url_param_index = if ident.text() == LINK_IDENT_ID {
    0  // standard link(): URL is always arg 0
} else if let Some(&idx) = wrappers.get(ident.text()) {
    idx
} else {
    return None;
};
```

Then when extracting the URL, use `extract_nth_string_arg(args, url_param_index)` instead of `extract_first_string_arg(args)`.

Add `extract_nth_string_arg(args: &SyntaxNode, n: usize) -> Option<(String, Range<usize>)>`:
- Counts positional args (skip `Named` arg nodes which are keyword args)
- Returns the n-th positional `Str` node's text (unquoted) AND its byte range relative to the source root

The `byte_range` stored in `LinkInfo` for wrapper calls must be the range of the `Str` node itself (not the whole FuncCall), so the serializer replaces only the string literal. To differentiate during serialization, `compute_transformations()` in `transformer.rs` must emit `ReplaceStringLiteralInPlace` when the transform source was a wrapper call. Add a `bool is_wrapper_call` field to `LinkInfo`, or detect it by checking whether the byte_range is smaller than the enclosing FuncCall.

Simplest approach: add `pub is_wrapper_call: bool` to `LinkInfo`. Set to `true` when extracted via wrapper map. In `transformer.rs::compute_transformations()`, use `ReplaceStringLiteralInPlace` when `link.is_wrapper_call` and the transform would otherwise be `ReplaceUrl`.

For `Remove` (single PDF) and `ReplaceUrlWithLabel` (merged PDF), wrapper-call links also need special handling:
- `Remove`: replace only the string arg, leave the wrapper call's structure intact, OR emit the body text — decide based on whether the full call can be removed. For simplicity: for `Remove`, replace the string with an empty string `""` (the wrapper function call remains but navigates nowhere). Document this as a known limitation.
- `ReplaceUrlWithLabel`: replace the string literal with the label string `"<chapter2>"` — note this changes a string arg to a label syntax, which may not be valid for all wrapper signatures. Document as a known limitation.

### Step 6 — Add tests in `parser.rs`

```rust
#[test]
fn test_wrapper_function_alias() {
    let source = Source::detached(r#"
        #let mylink = link
        #mylink("./chapter2.typ")[text]
    "#);
    let links = extract_links(&source);
    assert_eq!(links.len(), 1);
    assert_eq!(links[0].url, "./chapter2.typ");
    assert!(links[0].is_wrapper_call);
}

#[test]
fn test_wrapper_function_with_param() {
    let source = Source::detached(r#"
        #let chapter-ref(path, title) = link(path, title)
        #chapter-ref("./ch02.typ", [Chapter 2])
    "#);
    let links = extract_links(&source);
    assert_eq!(links.len(), 1);
    assert_eq!(links[0].url, "./ch02.typ");
}

#[test]
fn test_wrapper_call_after_definition_not_found() {
    // cross-file wrapper: function not defined in this source → silently skipped
    let source = Source::detached(r#"#chapter-ref("./ch02.typ", [Ch 2])"#);
    let links = extract_links(&source);
    assert_eq!(links.len(), 0);
}
```

Add matching end-to-end tests in `transformer.rs` verifying that `transform_source()` rewrites the URL in wrapper calls.

## Expected outcome

`#chapter-ref("./ch02.typ", ...)` in a source file that also defines `chapter-ref` as a `link()` wrapper produces a correctly rewritten URL (`./ch02.html` for HTML, etc.) after `transform_source()`.

## Known limitation

Cross-file wrappers (defined in an imported file, called in the current file) are not detected — the link will silently pass through untransformed. This will be addressed in a future issue by following `#import` statements during pre-pass analysis.

## Background

This is part of a feature to auto-detect Rheo packages from Typst import statements. After scanning .typ files for package imports (see companion issue), we need to resolve each import path string like `@rheo/slides:0.1.0` to a local filesystem directory. This must NOT download packages — it only checks if the package is already locally available.

Typst stores packages in two locations (data dir takes priority over cache dir):
- Data dir (Linux): `~/.local/share/typst/packages/{namespace}/{name}/{version}/`
- Cache dir (Linux): `~/.cache/typst/packages/{namespace}/{name}/{version}/`

These are obtained via `dirs::data_dir()` and `dirs::cache_dir()` (the `dirs` crate is already in Cargo.toml).

## Relevant existing code

- `crates/cli/src/lib.rs:637-644` — shows how the existing code uses `dirs::cache_dir().join("typst/packages")` for @preview package resolution
- `crates/core/src/plugins/mod.rs:268-271` — `ResolvedPackage { name: String, source_root: PathBuf }` (existing type for user-declared packages; this issue creates a NEW type `ImportedPackage` in manifest.rs)
- `crates/core/src/plugins/manifest.rs` — the file created in the companion issue (issue 1)

## Steps to implement

1. In `crates/core/src/plugins/manifest.rs`, add:

```rust
use std::path::PathBuf;

/// Parsed fields from a Typst package import string.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct ImportedPackage {
    pub namespace: String,
    pub name: String,
    pub version: String,
    pub source_root: PathBuf,
}

/// Given an import path like "@rheo/slides:0.1.0", parses namespace/name/version,
/// probes data dir then cache dir, and returns the resolved package directory.
/// Returns None silently if the path is unparseable or the package is not locally present.
pub fn find_local_package_dir(import_path: &str) -> Option<ImportedPackage> {
    let without_at = import_path.strip_prefix('@')?;
    let slash = without_at.find('/')?;
    let namespace = &without_at[..slash];
    let rest = &without_at[slash + 1..];
    let colon = rest.rfind(':')?;
    let name = &rest[..colon];
    let version = &rest[colon + 1..];
    if namespace.is_empty() || name.is_empty() || version.is_empty() {
        return None;
    }
    let rel = PathBuf::from(namespace).join(name).join(version);
    let candidates = [
        dirs::data_dir().map(|d| d.join("typst/packages").join(&rel)),
        dirs::cache_dir().map(|d| d.join("typst/packages").join(&rel)),
    ];
    let source_root = candidates.into_iter().flatten().find(|p| p.is_dir())?;
    Some(ImportedPackage {
        namespace: namespace.to_string(),
        name: name.to_string(),
        version: version.to_string(),
        source_root,
    })
}
